craps for OS-9/68000====================ORIG holds comp.sources.games volume 1, issue 9 (craps, 1987), as posted --seventeen files, verified against the posting byte for byte.  The filesbeside it build the OS-9 program.  It is K&R throughout and needed noconversion.  The high roller list is /dd/GAMES/CRAPS/craps.list.What changed, and why:  types.h      This curses.h defines REFRESH as 256, for the pad-refresh               macros craps does not use; craps means one of its own key               codes by that name, and cpp keeps the first definition, so               the redraw key could never match.  REFRESH is undefined               before craps defines it.               curses.l defines update() in its own psect and l68 takes a               second definition of the name as a clash, so craps's               update() is craps_update here.  final.c      The score file is /dd/GAMES/CRAPS/craps.list, and it ships               empty.  The lock was a link() of that file onto a second               name -- there is no link() here, and RBF has no hard links               at all -- so it is not taken; this disk is one player's.               The player's name came from $LOGNAME, which nothing sets               here: SYS/login sets USER, and craps_player() answers with               that, or "nobody".  $CRAPSNAME still wins if it is set.               It also included <sys/types.h> and <sys/file.h> for nothing               -- it opens the list with fopen().  THAT MATTERED: the               recipe searches the source directory first, craps has a               types.h of its own, and <sys/types.h> includes <types.h>,               so the program's own header answered and pulled <curses.h>               in a second time.  curses.h has no include guard, so               sgstat.h arrived twice and struct _sgs was defined twice --               62 errors, every one of them reported inside a system               header the program never names.  makeb.c      The key read was getchar(), and crmode() only sets a flag               in this curses -- it does not put the terminal in character               mode -- so stdio still waited for a whole line.  getch() is               the read that honours it, through craps_getkey() in subs.c,               which also calls check_signal() so ^C reaches the handler.               The shell escape forks /bin/csh; there is no fork() in this               C library, so it says so instead.  The cheat that sets the               dice is behind a crypt() password, and crypt() is in no               library here, so that key is refused.  types.h      printw() BUS-ERRORS on a floating-point conversion here,               and craps keeps every amount as a double, so it died               before drawing a character.  It is printw alone: measured               against wprintw() and mvwprintw(), which render the same               float correctly -- to stdscr as to any other window --               and against printw() of an integer, which is fine.  So               printw(f, a) is wprintw(stdscr, f, a) here, and all               twenty-seven call sites stand as they were posted.  The               macro takes one argument after the format, which is what               every one of them passes.prints.c needs no change either, and neither does random.c: its XENIX armis time(), srand() and rand(), which is exactly what this C library has,and the recipe defines XENIX.dont.c, etc.c, help.c, main.c, pass.c, pay.c and props.c are unchanged.Measured under os9exec: the table draws with its dice, the field and theprop bets, the rack reads 100.00, `?' lists every key without a Returnbeing needed, and `q' leaves the game.Build: cc main.c dont.c etc.c help.c makeb.c pass.c pay.c prints.c props.c          subs.c final.c random.c -DOSK -DXENIX -DSCORES -l=curses.l          -l=termlib.l -l=math.l